  <p>We now have a continuum set of nonlinear auxiliary one-particle equations that produces the density predicted by a declared approximate exchange-correlation functional. But a computer cannot store continuous orbitals, potentials, or Brillouin-zone integrals exactly. Which finite objects represent them, and how can numerical solution error be kept separate from the physical approximation?</p>

